is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The suspected fault was an OSPF virtual-link collapse between r4 and r8 (carried through Area 1) that would isolate Area 2 from the backbone. On inspection, the OSPF control-plane is healthy and the virtual-link is currently established.

Evidence:
- r4 and r8 both have matching virtual-link configuration under router ospf (r4: “area 1 virtual-link 18.1.4.1”; r8: “area 1 virtual-link 14.1.4.1”).
- Each router shows the virtual-link adjacency up and in Full state over interface VLINK0 in Area 0:
  - r4: Neighbor 18.1.4.1 in area 0 via VLINK0, State Full.
  - r8: Neighbor 14.1.4.1 in area 0 via VLINK0, State Full.
- Both routers report “Number of full virtual adjacencies going through this area: 1” for Area 1.
- Inter-area reachability to Area 2 prefixes is present network-wide:
  - r5 has OSPF route to 80.0.0.0/8 via 58.1.1.2 (r8).
  - r6 has 80.0.0.0/8 via r5.
  - r4 has 80.0.0.0/8 installed (next-hop via Area 1 toward r5), indicating Area 2 LSAs are being learned through the VL.
- End-to-end host reachability (pc0 ↔ pc12) shows no loss.

Mechanism: If the VL had failed, Area 2 inter-area routes would vanish or black-hole. Instead, LSAs and routes are present, and the VL adjacency is Full on both sides.

Root cause: No ongoing fault; the VL likely briefly flapped during the reload but recovered.

Corrective fix: None required. Recommend tightening monitoring to alert only if the r4–r8 VL stays down beyond a grace period and verifying router-ID persistence to avoid transient alarms after reloads.
=== END ===